# shred — real-world examples

## Overwrite a file 5 times with verbose output

```sh
shred -n 5 -v secrets.txt
```

## Overwrite, then a final pass of zeroes to hide that it was shredded

```sh
shred -n 5 -vz secrets.txt
```

## Overwrite, then truncate and remove the file

```sh
shred -n 5 -vzu secrets.txt
```

## Wipe an old drive before it leaves the homelab

```sh
sudo shred -n 3 -vz /dev/sdb
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | What it does                                    |
| ---- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| -n N | overwrite N times                               |
| -v   | show progress                                   |
| -z   | add a final overwrite of zeroes                 |
| -u   | truncate and remove the file after overwriting  |

> Note: shred may not fully destroy data on journaling, copy-on-write, or
> flash-based (SSD/USB) filesystems where the original blocks can be relocated.
> For those, prefer full-device encryption or the drive's secure-erase command.
